        "Alexandre Blais",
        "Robert J. Schoelkopf",
        "Steven M. Girvin"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Circuit QED established a powerful architecture for coupling superconducting artificial atoms to microwave fields, enabling control of individual quantum systems and a broad program in quantum optics and information.",
        "attribution": "Blais's theoretical proposal, Schoelkopf's experimental development, and Girvin's theoretical contributions form a strong core, though the platform depended on contributions from many groups.",
        "maturity": "The field has been experimentally robust for decades and has produced extensive independent results. Its scientific importance stands apart from whether scalable quantum computing is achieved.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The boundary between this contribution and the broader development of superconducting qubits recognized in 2025 needs careful definition."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B004",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Hidetoshi Katori",
        "Jun Ye"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks transformed precision timekeeping by combining optical transitions with lattice trapping that suppresses key systematic shifts; they also enable tests of fundamental physics.",
        "attribution": "Katori and Ye made complementary foundational contributions to the clock concept and its experimental realization.",
        "maturity": "The approach is well established, independently reproduced, and continues to improve in accuracy and applications.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The eventual prize framing should distinguish the core clock invention from subsequent metrology advances by a large community."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B003",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Markus Aspelmeyer",
        "Tobias J. Kippenberg",
        "Kerry J. Vahala"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Cavity optomechanics created a general way to couple light to mechanical motion, enabling precise control of mesoscopic oscillators and new tests at the boundary of quantum and classical physics.",
        "attribution": "Vahala established important optical resonator foundations, while Kippenberg and Aspelmeyer helped develop the field and demonstrate quantum control; the work is international and collective.",
        "maturity": "The experimental field is mature, with strong replication and expanding quantum control. The deepest tests of macroscopic quantum behavior remain in progress.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The long-term foundational impact of mechanical quantum control relative to its precision measurement and sensing uses is still developing."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B027",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Michael Berry",
        "Yakir Aharonov"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The Aharonov–Bohm and Berry phases revealed that quantum phase has observable, geometric structure with consequences across quantum mechanics, condensed matter, and optics.",
        "attribution": "Aharonov and Berry are appropriately associated with distinct but related foundational insights; the wider field built on both results.",
        "maturity": "The effects have been tested and applied across many systems for decades, and their conceptual importance is secure.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The proposed scope joins two related discoveries, and the prize citation would need to make their distinct contributions clear."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B030",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Eli Yablonovitch",
        "Sajeev John",
        "Philip St.J. Russell"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Photonic crystals made control of light through engineered band structures a practical and influential part of optics, opening routes to confine, guide, and shape electromagnetic modes.",
        "attribution": "Yablonovitch and John independently articulated central photonic band-gap ideas; Russell's photonic-crystal fibers extended the concept into a major optical platform.",
        "maturity": "The concepts have been validated in many structures and have had lasting research and technological influence.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The proposed grouping spans photonic band-gap physics and photonic-crystal fiber development, whose relationship and individual credit require a focused citation."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B035",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Carlton M. Caves",
        "H. Jeff Kimble",
        "Roman Schnabel"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Squeezed light applies quantum-state engineering to precision measurement, overcoming measurement noise limits and improving interferometric detection.",
        "attribution": "Caves supplied key theoretical insight, Kimble helped establish experimental quantum optics methods, and Schnabel has led important precision-measurement demonstrations.",
        "maturity": "The physics is mature and the technique has demonstrated practical value, including in gravitational-wave observatories.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The discovery, experimental development, and deployment span several generations and a broad community, making a narrow attribution difficult."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B047",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Immanuel Bloch",
        "Markus Greiner",
        "Tilman Esslinger"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical-lattice quantum simulators gave experimental access to controlled many-body regimes that are difficult to study in conventional materials and made atomic gases a versatile platform for quantum matter.",
        "attribution": "Bloch, Greiner, and Esslinger led complementary experiments that established the platform and demonstrated important many-body phenomena; Zoller also made foundational theoretical contributions but is not included here.",
        "maturity": "The platform is robust and has generated extensive results, though its role as a quantitatively predictive simulator for complex materials is still being established.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "It remains uncertain which discoveries from quantum simulation will prove most broadly consequential.",
          "The proposed laureate set is necessarily selective in a field with substantial theoretical and experimental contributions."
        ]
      }
    },
